---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const publishDate = '2026-08-30';
const title = '300 Free Developer Tools: What We Built and Why';
const description = 'Why we built a single site with 300 no-signup developer tools instead of another SaaS product, and what actually shipped.';
const canonicalPath = '/blog/300-free-developer-tools';

const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BlogPosting',
  headline: title,
  description,
  datePublished: publishDate,
  author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'DevToolkit' },
};
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} />
  <div class="max-w-2xl mx-auto px-4 py-16 prose prose-slate dark:prose-invert">
    <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 dark:text-slate-500 mb-1">August 30, 2026</p>
    <p class="text-sm text-emerald-600 dark:text-emerald-400 font-medium mb-2">Blog</p>
    <h1>{title}</h1>

    <p>
      Most developer tool sites pick one problem and solve it well — a JSON formatter, a regex
      tester, a UUID generator. We started the same way, building individual tools for problems we
      kept hitting ourselves. At some point the list got long enough that it made sense to put them
      all in one place rather than bookmarking twenty different single-purpose sites.
    </p>

    <h2>Why 300, and why free</h2>
    <p>
      The number 300 wasn't a target we set out to hit — it's just where things landed after
      covering the categories that come up constantly in day-to-day development: working with AI
      APIs, formatting and validating JSON, writing SQL, managing git, securing an application, and
      the handful of business calculations that inevitably land on a developer's desk when they're
      also running a small project or freelancing.
    </p>
    <p>
      Everything runs client-side in your browser wherever possible — no account, no data sent to a
      server, no rate limit. That wasn't a marketing decision so much as the obvious way to build a
      tool you'd actually want to use for a two-second task. Signing up for an account to format a
      JSON blob is a genuinely bad experience, and we didn't want to build that.
    </p>

    <h2>What's actually useful right now</h2>
    <p>
      A few tools get disproportionate use based on what we hear back: the
      <a href="/tools/json-formatter">JSON Formatter</a> and <a href="/tools/jwt-decoder">JWT Decoder</a>
      for quick debugging, the <a href="/tools/api-pricing-calculator">API Pricing Calculator</a> for
      anyone trying to estimate what an AI feature will actually cost before building it, and the
      <a href="/tools/uuid-generator">UUID Generator</a> and <a href="/tools/password-generator">Password Generator</a>
      for the kind of quick, low-stakes generation task that doesn't deserve its own bookmark.
    </p>

    <h2>What's next</h2>
    <p>
      We're actively expanding the <a href="/guides">guides section</a> — longer pieces that connect
      several tools around a real workflow, like understanding AI API costs end to end or designing
      a REST API without the common mistakes that only surface after external consumers depend on
      it. The tool count will keep growing too, based mostly on what people ask for.
    </p>
    <p>
      If there's a tool you wish existed, the <a href="/contact">contact page</a> is the fastest way
      to tell us.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
